---
import QualityLayout from "../../../layouts/QualityLayout.astro";
import {
  auditCases,
  baselineCases,
  categoryCounts,
  categoryLabel,
  comparisonSummary,
  totalCases,
  totalRecords,
} from "../../../data/quality-2026";

const fastFixes = auditCases.filter((item) => item.responseDays <= 5).length;
const baselineFastFixes = baselineCases.filter((item) => item.responseDays <= 5).length;
---

<QualityLayout
  title="Counterfact 2026 Quality Audit"
  description="An evidence-backed comparison of confirmed external Counterfact product-defect reports in matching 2025 and 2026 windows."
  canonicalPath="/quality/2026"
  active="overview"
>
  <section class="quality-hero">
    <div class="quality-wide">
      <p class="quality-kicker">Independent reports · traced through source history</p>
      <h1>What Counterfact’s 2026 bug reports actually show</h1>
      <p class="quality-deck">
        Ten confirmed product defects were reported by external users through September 3.
        Eight predated 2026. Two arrived inside a feature first released in 2026. None
        was a regression from previously working behavior caused by a 2026 change. The
        same screen found four product defects in the matching 2025 window, all pre-existing.
      </p>
      <p class="quality-dateline">
        Audit period: January 1–September 3, 2026 · Published September 3, 2026
      </p>
      <div class="quality-hero-actions">
        <a class="quality-button quality-button-primary" href="/quality/2026/cases">
          Explore the case files
        </a>
        <a class="quality-button quality-button-secondary" href="/quality/2026/methodology">
          Review the method
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="quality-section quality-wide" aria-labelledby="result-heading">
    <p class="quality-section-label">The result</p>
    <h2 id="result-heading">A lopsided historical distribution</h2>
    <p class="quality-section-intro">
      Each confirmed defect received exactly one primary classification. The categories
      describe when the faulty behavior entered the released product—not when it was
      noticed or fixed. The adjacent 2025 baseline uses identical report dates and rules.
    </p>
    <div class="quality-result-grid">
      {
        categoryCounts.map((category) => (
          <article class="quality-result-card" data-tone={category.tone}>
            <span class="quality-result-number">{category.count}</span>
            <span class="quality-result-percent">{category.percent}% of confirmed bugs</span>
            <span class="quality-result-label">{categoryLabel(category.label, 2026)}</span>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <div class="quality-callout">
      <strong>The bounded conclusion:</strong> the 2026 cohort contains no same-year product
      regression, including when limited to releases with at least 90 complete days of
      observation. It does contain two defects born with a 2026 feature and one separate
      release-provenance incident. These observations do not establish what caused the pattern.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="quality-section quality-wide" aria-labelledby="comparison-heading">
    <p class="quality-section-label">Matched comparison</p>
    <h2 id="comparison-heading">Same rules, same dates, side by side</h2>
    <p class="quality-section-intro">
      The comparison is descriptive rather than causal. It answers how the two public-report
      cohorts differ; it cannot isolate the effect of agents, review, testing, usage, or product mix.
    </p>
    <table class="quality-metric-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Measure</th>
          {comparisonSummary.map((summary) => <th scope="col">{summary.year}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Confirmed product defects</th>{comparisonSummary.map((summary) => <td>{summary.total}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Same-year regressions</th>{comparisonSummary.map((summary) => <td>{summary.regressions}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Defects born with a same-year feature</th>{comparisonSummary.map((summary) => <td>{summary.featureDefects}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Pre-existing defects</th>{comparisonSummary.map((summary) => <td>{summary.preExisting}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">90-day mature-release sensitivity</th>
          {comparisonSummary.map((summary) => <td>{summary.matureTotal} included</td>)}
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="quality-note">
      Issue #2348 is recorded separately as a 2026 process-quality incident and is excluded
      from both product-defect columns.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="quality-section">
    <div class="quality-wide quality-split">
      <div class="quality-prose">
        <p class="quality-section-label">Why the finding matters</p>
        <h2>The product got much busier; the defect mix did not shift toward regressions</h2>
        <p>
          The result did not emerge from a quiet maintenance year. By September 3,
          Counterfact had published 28 releases—3.1 times the nine published in the same
          period of 2025—and merged 625 pull requests, 2.8 times the prior-year count.
          The product added request validation, reusable scenarios, multiple APIs and
          versions, substantial OpenAPI 3.2 support, Overlays, a shared store, and modular
          packages for embedding.
        </p>
        <p>
          Testing expanded at the same time. A consistent source scan finds 72 test files
          on the September 3 main branch, up from 35 at the final 2025 release, and 904
          explicit test declarations, up from 240. Main-branch line coverage reported by
          Coveralls rose from 84.26% on that release to 91.73%, even as coverage broadened
          from the former single-package source tree to six packages.
        </p>
        <p>
          Adoption signals also moved upward: npm recorded 62,132 downloads from January
          through August, 12.2% more than the same months in 2025, while GitHub stars rose
          from 131 at the end of 2025 to 168 on the audit date. These are imperfect proxies,
          but they show that the small defect count was not produced by disappearing use.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/quality/2026/context">See the full delivery, testing, usage, and feature context →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="quality-stat-list" aria-label="Audit facts">
        <div>
          <dt>2026 product defects</dt>
          <dd>{totalCases}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>2026 released within five days</dt>
          <dd>{fastFixes} of {totalCases}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>2025 baseline defects</dt>
          <dd>{baselineCases.length}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>2025 released within five days</dt>
          <dd>{baselineFastFixes} of {baselineCases.length}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Same-year regressions</dt>
          <dd>0 in either cohort</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Process incidents</dt>
          <dd>1, outside product percentages</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="quality-section quality-wide" aria-labelledby="map-heading">
    <p class="quality-section-label">Read the evidence</p>
    <h2 id="map-heading">A report designed to be inspected</h2>
    <p class="quality-section-intro">
      The summary is intentionally short. The supporting material is separated by the
      question a reader is trying to answer.
    </p>
    <div class="quality-link-grid">
      <a class="quality-link-card" href="/quality/2026/cases">
        <span>{totalRecords} records</span>
        <h3>Case files</h3>
        <p>
          Ten 2026 defects, four complete 2025 baseline cases, and one release-provenance
          incident, each with source, chronology, release, and confidence details.
        </p>
      </a>
      <a class="quality-link-card" href="/quality/2026/context">
        <span>Year-over-year context</span>
        <h3>Delivery and adoption</h3>
        <p>
          Release velocity, pull-request volume, test growth, coverage, usage signals, and
          a timeline of the year’s major features.
        </p>
      </a>
      <a class="quality-link-card" href="/quality/2026/methodology">
        <span>Rules and limits</span>
        <h3>Methodology</h3>
        <p>
          Population criteria, classification rules, evidence standard, validation steps,
          and what this audit cannot establish.
        </p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="quality-section">
    <div class="quality-reading quality-prose">
      <p class="quality-section-label">Synthesis</p>
      <h2>The pattern is older edge cases, found and closed quickly</h2>
      <p>
        Seven 2026 cases concerned code generation or interpretation of OpenAPI details: nested
        required properties, reserved identifiers, schema-less responses, colon-bearing
        routes, Path Item metadata, and path-level parameters. Two concerned runtime request
        behavior: root middleware and exploded object query parameters. One concerned package
        installation hygiene.
      </p>
      <p>
        The concentration is technically coherent. Counterfact sits at a difficult boundary
        between a broad specification, generated TypeScript, filesystem-safe route names,
        and live HTTP behavior. Most externally discovered failures were unusual but valid
        inputs reaching assumptions that had survived for years. Once the reproductions
        arrived, nine of ten 2026 corrections reached a public release within five calendar days.
      </p>
      <p>
        The strongest defensible story is therefore not “no bugs” or “AI caused quality.”
        It is that a faster, AI-assisted development period coincided with expanded capability
        and verification, while this defined external-report population contains no same-year
        regression. The process incident and the two feature defects remain part of that record.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</QualityLayout>
